WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.316
January 17

2
00:00:02.317 --> 00:00:04.773
Dear Daddy Longlegs,

3
00:00:04.774 --> 00:00:08.613
Today is a very rainy day!

4
00:00:08.614 --> 00:00:12.972
I like winter to act like winter with snow, not rain.

5
00:00:12.973 --> 00:00:18.494
I still managed to have a good day because Julia's uncle came to visit again.

6
00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:23.372
This time he brought a five-pound box of chocolates!

7
00:00:23.373 --> 00:00:27.068
There are advantages to being Julia's roommate.

8
00:00:27.069 --> 00:00:30.058
We had to ask permission to entertain him.

9
00:00:30.059 --> 00:00:33.634
It's a good thing the dean didn't see Uncle Jervie.

10
00:00:33.635 --> 00:00:40.257
If she knew how young and handsome he is, she'd never let him set foot in our study!

11
00:00:40.258 --> 00:00:43.867
The three of us seemed to amuse Uncle Jervie.

12
00:00:43.868 --> 00:00:49.647
In fact, he decided to catch a later train, so he could have tea with us.

13
00:00:49.648 --> 00:00:53.286
I started to make cheese sandwiches on brown bread.

14
00:00:53.287 --> 00:00:56.718
"Let me help you," Uncle Jervie said.

15
00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:05.309
I handed him a knife, and he sliced the cheese.

16
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.419
Then we sat around eating sandwiches and drinking tea.

17
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.606
"I spent last summer at Lock Willow Farm," I said, passing him a cup of tea.

18
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:18.848
"Did you? I have many happy memories of that place."

19
00:01:18.849 --> 00:01:25.530
We had a wonderful time gossiping about the Semples and all the farm animals.

20
00:01:25.531 --> 00:01:31.184
"Mrs. Semple seems awfully fond of you, Master Jervie," I said.

21
00:01:31.185 --> 00:01:36.089
He smiled when I called him Master Jervie , but he didn't seem to

22
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.124
mind. "And I'm very fond of

23
00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:41.690
her. The last time I was there, the Semples had a new

24
00:01:41.691 --> 00:01:44.339
colt. Now what was its

25
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.920
name?" He took a bite of

26
00:01:46.921 --> 00:01:49.717
sandwich. "Oh, I remember.

27
00:01:49.718 --> 00:01:56.100
Grover." I laughed. "You haven't been there for ages

28
00:01:56.101 --> 00:02:01.972
then! Grover is so old that he can barely limp around the

29
00:02:01.973 --> 00:02:04.294
pasture." "Well, it was a long time

30
00:02:04.295 --> 00:02:08.590
ago. Does Mrs. Semple still keep doughnuts on the bottom shelf of the

31
00:02:08.591 --> 00:02:13.423
pantry?" "Yes! In a yellow pot with a blue plate on

32
00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:15.835
top." "That's how she stored them when I was a

33
00:02:15.836 --> 00:02:18.715
child. Let's see what else I

34
00:02:18.716 --> 00:02:21.255
remember." He took a sip of

35
00:02:21.256 --> 00:02:26.088
tea. "Is there still a groundhog hole under the pile of rocks in the far

36
00:02:26.089 --> 00:02:31.718
pasture?" I nodded. "And there are still groundhogs living

37
00:02:31.719 --> 00:02:38.987
there! They must be the great-great-great-great-great-great grandchildren of the ones you used to

38
00:02:38.988 --> 00:02:42.746
see." So we chatted like that for the rest of

39
00:02:42.747 --> 00:02:49.732
teatime. He talked mostly to me, leaving Julia and Sallie to talk to each

40
00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:55.908
other. After he left, Julia said, "I've never seen my uncle in such a good mood.

41
00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:59.768
He's usually so aloof that I'm afraid to approach him."

42
00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:03.373
I think that's partly Julia's fault.

43
00:03:03.374 --> 00:03:06.037
She doesn't know how to talk to men.

44
00:03:06.038 --> 00:03:10.806
By the way, Uncle Jervie ate four cheese sandwiches.

45
00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:11.947
Yours truly,

46
00:03:11.948 --> 00:03:14.538
Judy

47
00:03:14.539 --> 00:03:16.040
February 4

48
00:03:16.041 --> 00:03:18.304
Dear Daddy Longlegs,

49
00:03:18.305 --> 00:03:22.668
Jimmy McBride sent me a huge Princeton banner.

50
00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:25.452
My roommates are not happy about it.

51
00:03:25.453 --> 00:03:30.381
"I'm very glad he remembered me," I said, unfolding the banner.

52
00:03:30.382 --> 00:03:33.004
"But I don't know what to do with it.

53
00:03:33.005 --> 00:03:35.846
Look at the size of this thing!

54
00:03:35.847 --> 00:03:39.319
It's as big as one end of our study."

55
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.990
"Well, you're not hanging it in here," Sallie said.

56
00:03:43.991 --> 00:03:48.865
"It really clashes with our decor," Julia said.

57
00:03:48.866 --> 00:03:52.306
I had to agree with that.

58
00:03:52.307 --> 00:03:57.612
Can you imagine if I added a giant orange and black banner?

59
00:03:57.613 --> 00:04:01.008
"But it's such nice, warm, thick felt."

60
00:04:01.009 --> 00:04:02.794
I patted the banner.

61
00:04:02.795 --> 00:04:05.083
"I hate to waste it."

62
00:04:05.084 --> 00:04:10.624
"You're not hanging it in here," Sallie repeated firmly.

63
00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:15.078
So now I have to figure out what to do.

64
00:04:15.079 --> 00:04:20.328
Do you think it's improper to have Jimmy's banner made into a bathrobe?

65
00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:23.705
My old one shrank in the wash.

66
00:04:23.706 --> 00:04:26.714
I realize I haven't mentioned my studies.

67
00:04:26.715 --> 00:04:31.896
Sometimes it's confusing to study five different subjects at once.

68
00:04:31.897 --> 00:04:39.999
"The mark of a true scientist is a passion for detail," my chemistry professor says.

69
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.102
"Don't keep your eyes glued to the details," my history professor says.

70
00:04:46.103 --> 00:04:50.375
"You must look at the broader view."

71
00:04:50.376 --> 00:04:54.221
I hope I can remember which one wants what.

72
00:04:54.222 --> 00:04:57.757
Exams are next week, but who's afraid?

73
00:04:57.758 --> 00:04:58.839
Your student,

74
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.440
Judy

75
00:05:01.441 --> 00:05:02.736
March 5

76
00:05:02.737 --> 00:05:05.034
Dear Daddy Longlegs,

77
00:05:05.035 --> 00:05:08.044
I never told you about exams.

78
00:05:08.045 --> 00:05:11.429
I passed everything. Phew!

79
00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:16.426
I know the secrets of studying now, so I'm never going to fail again.

80
00:05:16.427 --> 00:05:22.933
This week I'm reading Hamlet for my Shakespeare class, which continues this semester.

81
00:05:22.934 --> 00:05:26.968
It's WONDERFUL!

82
00:05:26.969 --> 00:05:30.765
Lying in bed at night, I pretend I'm Ophelia.

83
00:05:30.766 --> 00:05:33.732
But I change some things in the play.

84
00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:38.906
I keep Hamlet amused all the time and make him dress warmly.

85
00:05:38.907 --> 00:05:42.574
As a result, he's a happy fellow.

86
00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:44.927
We're ruling Denmark.

87
00:05:44.928 --> 00:05:48.565
The kingdom is running beautifully.

88
00:05:48.566 --> 00:05:52.652
Hamlet governs while I'm in charge of the charities.

89
00:05:52.653 --> 00:05:56.087
I've just built some first-class orphanages.

90
00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:59.695
Let me know if you and the other trustees want to visit.

91
00:05:59.696 --> 00:06:04.184
I'm sure you'll pick up some useful ideas for the John Grier Home.

92
00:06:04.185 --> 00:06:05.881
Yours most graciously,

93
00:06:05.882 --> 00:06:11.689
OPHELIA, Queen of Denmark

